# KNOWN GAP repro — the self-host's `infer_expr_type` returns NO_TYPE for a
# FIELD READ whose receiver is a METHOD CALL, because the `EFieldAccess` arm in
# `tests/fixtures/self_host_typechecker/infer.gg` only handles an `RTDefined`
# receiver and `nodes.get(i).unwrap()` is an `RTRef(Node)` — there is no
# `RTRef` arm, so it falls through to `return NO_TYPE`.
#
# INTENDED: this program is REJECTED with
#   error[E_NonExhaustiveMatch]: non-exhaustive match: missing variants: Halt
# on BOTH lanes. Rust `gg check` already does (it peels the ref). The self-host
# ACCEPTS it and lowers ~7.8 KB of C, because the exhaustiveness check
# conservatively bails when the scrutinee type is unknown.
#
# MEASURED (self-host driver, `--lir-c`):
#   `n.kind`                        (field off a LOCAL)        -> typed, rejects
#   `ks.get(i).unwrap()`            (method call, no field)    -> typed, rejects
#   `nodes[i].kind`                 (field off an INDEX)       -> typed, rejects
#   `nodes.get(i).unwrap().kind`    (field off a METHOD CALL)  -> NO_TYPE, ACCEPTS
#
# The same shape is already pinned on the Rust lane by
# `tests/fixtures/field_off_element_match_nonexhaustive_error.gg`; this repro is
# the self-host half. The gap is NOT in `check_match_exhaustiveness` — it is one
# layer up, at the write site, and it silently disables every type-directed
# check on this expression shape, not just exhaustiveness.
enum Kind:
    Value()
    Next()
    Halt()

struct Node:
    Kind kind
    int payload

int walk(Vector[Node] nodes, int i):
    match nodes.get(i).unwrap().kind:
        case Kind.Value():
            return 1
        case Kind.Next():
            return 2

void main():
    Vector[Node] a = [Node(Kind.Value(), 1)]
    print(walk(a, 0))
